---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const canonical = "https://octiflow.app/editorial-standards/";
const structuredData = {
  "@type": "WebPage",
  "@id": `${canonical}#webpage`,
  url: canonical,
  name: "Editorial Standards | Octi Flow",
  description:
    "How EvaMariaDreams researches, writes, sources and updates health and hydration articles for the Octi Flow blog.",
  inLanguage: "en",
  isPartOf: {
    "@id": "https://octiflow.app/blog/#blog",
  },
  publisher: {
    "@id": "https://octiflow.app/#organization",
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Standards | Octi Flow"
  description="How EvaMariaDreams researches, writes, sources and updates health and hydration articles for the Octi Flow blog."
  {canonical}
  {structuredData}
>
  <main class="article-page blog-page-surface">
    <header class="article-hero">
      <div class="container article-hero__inner">
        <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">Octi Flow</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          <a href="/blog/">Blog</a>
        </nav>
        <p class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">How we work</p>
        <h1 class="blog-gradient-title">Editorial standards</h1>
        <p class="article-deck">
          How we research, source, write and update health and hydration articles
          on the Octi Flow blog.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container editorial-standards-page__content">
      <article class="article-prose">
        <p>
          Octi Flow is made by EvaMariaDreams, an independent two-person software
          studio. We are not a medical organisation, and our articles are not a
          substitute for personal medical care. When we publish health-related
          information, our job is to represent the evidence accurately and make
          its limits clear.
        </p>

        <h2>Our source hierarchy</h2>
        <p>For health and hydration claims, we prefer sources in this order:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Current guidance from public health agencies and professional medical organisations.</li>
          <li>Systematic reviews, meta-analyses and consensus statements.</li>
          <li>Peer-reviewed clinical trials and observational studies when they answer a narrower question.</li>
          <li>High-quality patient information from established medical institutions when it helps explain practical context.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Sources commonly used on this site include the NHS, WHO, CDC, EFSA,
          National Academies, NIH and NIDDK, PubMed-indexed research, and relevant
          professional societies. We do not use marketing pages, anonymous health
          blogs or social media posts as evidence for medical claims.
        </p>

        <h2>How we handle uncertainty</h2>
        <p>
          A plausible idea is not presented as a proven fact. If evidence is weak,
          mixed, indirect or based on a small study, we say so. Results from one
          study are not automatically turned into a general recommendation.
          Population guidance is not presented as an exact prescription for every
          individual.
        </p>
        <p>
          We also distinguish total water intake from plain drinking water, general
          daily hydration from exercise or illness, and advice for healthy adults
          from guidance intended for a clinical condition or higher-risk group.
        </p>

        <h2>Numbers need context</h2>
        <p>
          Hydration numbers can be misleading when their original context is lost.
          Whenever practical, we identify whether a figure refers to total water,
          beverages only, a particular population, exercise, occupational heat
          exposure, or treatment of a medical condition.
        </p>

        <h2>Medical review and authorship</h2>
        <p>
          Articles are written and edited by EvaMariaDreams. We do not label an
          article as medically reviewed unless a qualified healthcare professional
          has actually reviewed that specific article. At present, the blog does
          not make that claim.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections and updates</h2>
        <p>
          Health guidance and research can change. When a substantive article is
          revised, we may show an updated date. If a cited source is superseded or
          a claim is no longer supported, we aim to correct or remove it rather
          than preserve outdated wording for consistency.
        </p>

        <h2>What the blog is for</h2>
        <p>
          The blog is educational. It can explain general evidence, help readers
          understand common hydration concepts, and show where an app such as Octi
          Flow fits into habit tracking. It cannot diagnose dehydration, determine
          an individual's medical fluid requirement, or replace advice from a
          clinician who knows that person's health history.
        </p>

        <p class="editorial-standards-page__reviewed">
          Last reviewed: September 13, 2026.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
